ng `p`, or `n` when none does. -/\\n62: def minUpTo (n : ℕ) (p : ℕ → Prop) [DecidablePred p] : ℕ :=\\n63:   (((range (n + 1)).filter p).min).getD n\\n64: \\n65: /- ── order / size ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\\n66: \\n67: def order (_G : SimpleGraph V) : ℕ := Fintype.card V\\n68: \\n69: def size (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ := G.edgeFinset.card\\n70: \\n71: /- ── independence / clique ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\\n72: \\n73: /-- `s` is independent: no two of its vertices are adjacent. -/\\n74: def IsIndependentFinset (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (s : Finset V) : Prop :=\\n75:   ∀ u ∈ s, ∀ w ∈ s, ¬ G.Adj u w\\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\\n\"}\n27: {\"step_index\":70,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:19Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Evaluating graph definitions**\\n\\nThe code defines `minUpTo`, `eccentricity`, and `radius` for a simple graph. The `eccentricity` uses `minUpTo` with a bound related to the graph's vertex count.  A potential point to examine is the specific use of  `Fintype.card V` as the upper bound `n` in `minUpTo` when defining eccentricity. This could be crucial to the correctness and efficiency of the radius calculation.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing graph radius and domination**\\n\\nSince `HasRadius G` implies all vertices are within a radius equal to the graph's cardinality, the `eccentricity` is the smallest such radius for all vertices, therefore a valid radius. The minimum of `eccentricity` across all vertices defines the `radius G`.\\n\\nNow, considering `totalDominationNumber`, because `HasTotalDominationNumber G` has a witness, the filter over the powerset is nonempty. This means a total dominating set exists and the minimum size defines the `totalDominationNumber`.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"run_command\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/agy.log\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep run_command in agy.log\\\"\"}}]}\n28: {\"step_index\":71,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:23Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:46:23+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:46:24+02:00\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/agy.log\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":355,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"E0911 20:41:49.879726    5396 run_command_handler.go:699] error during input detection model call: request failed: Post \\\\\\\"https://daily-cloudcode-pa.googleapis.com/v1internal:streamGenerateContent?alt=sse\\\\\\\": context canceled\\\"}\"}\n29: {\"step_index\":72,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:24Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Exploring Lean's Graph Verification**\\n\\nThe primary Lean verification tool seems useful, but it requires confirmation if Lean itself can verify concrete graph properties. Lean *can* evaluate graph characteristics directly using `#eval` or `#decide`, offering a potential method. The next step is testing `#decide` on a concrete graph within a Lean file or test theorem.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"200\",\"StartLine\":\"100\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing file\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View lint in antigravity_handoff.py\\\"\"}}]}\n30: {\"step_index\":73,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:26Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:46:26+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:46:26+02:00\\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py`\\nTotal Lines: 348\\nTotal Bytes: 15551\\nShowing lines 100 to 200\\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\\n100: \\n101: \\n102: # ── admissibility ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\n103: #\\n104: # An answer is compiled as the *returned file*, helper lemmas included — pasting\\n105: # only the proof body back into the original, as the model campaign did, drops\\n106: # every lemma a real proof needs and turns a correct proof into \\\"unresolved\\\".\\n107: #\\n108: # Compiling the returned file is only sound if nothing in it can change what is\\n109: # being proved. The checks below close the routes that exist in Lean for that:\\n110: # editing `Candidate` or anything it elaborates against; extending its term past\\n111: # where it ended (a term does not stop at a newline); re-declaring `Candidate` or\\n112: # the target inside a namespace; adding `variable` hypotheses the target silently\\n113: # absorbs; redefining the tokens `Candidate` parses as; and bypassing the kernel.\\n114: # The target's type is then re-checked by Lean itself, and the axiom audit rejects\\n115: # `sorryAx` and anything non-standard.\\n116: \\n117: _CMD_START = (\\\"theorem\\\", \\\"lemma\\\", \\\"def\\\", \\\"abbrev\\\", \\\"example\\\", \\\"instance\\\", \\\"open\\\",\\n118:               \\\"set_option\\\", \\\"attribute\\\", \\\"noncomputable\\\", \\\"private\\\", \\\"protected\\\",\\n119:               \\\"@[\\\", \\\"#check\\\", \\\"#print\\\", \\\"structure\\\", \\\"inductive\\\", \\\"class\\\")\\n120: \\n121: _FORBIDDEN_CMD = re.compile(\\n122:     r\\\"(?m)^\\\\s*(?:(?:private|protected|noncomputable|local|scoped)\\\\s+)*\\\"\\n123:     r\\\"(namespace|section|variable|universe|notati\\n<truncated 1911 bytes>\\nn): may the returned file be compiled as an answer at all?\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n162:     orig, ret = _norm(original), _norm(returned)\\n163:     pre = _prefix(orig)\\n164:     if pre is None:\\n165:         return False, \\\"original challenge is malformed\\\"\\n166:     if not ret.startswith(pre):\\n167:         return False, \\\"statement changed: text above the first theorem differs\\\"\\n168:     rest = ret[len(pre):]\\n169:     if rest and not rest.startswith(\\\"\\\\n\\\"):\\n170:         return False, \\\"statement changed: Candidate's last line was extended\\\"\\n171:     code = _strip_comments(rest)\\n172:     head = code.lstrip()\\n173:     if head and not head.startswith(_CMD_START):\\n174:         return False, \\\"statement changed: Candidate continues past its original end\\\"\\n175:     m = _FORBIDDEN_CMD.search(code)\\n176:     if m:\\n177:         return False, f\\\"forbidden command `{m.group(1)}`\\\"\\n178:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\\n179:     if m:\\n180:         return False, f\\\"forbidden construct `{m.group(0)}`\\\"\\n181:     return True, \\\"\\\"\\n182: \\n183: \\n184: def lint_text(text: str, original=None):\\n185:     \\\"\\\"\\\"(ok, reason) for any Lean file an agent asks to elaborate.\\n186: \\n187:     A challenge file is held to `structural_check` against its original. Any\\n188:     other file (scratch lemmas) gets the same code-execution screen plus an\\n189:     import whitelist, since `import` is the other way to pull in code.\\n190:     \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n191:     if original is not None:\\n192:         return structural_check(original, text)\\n193:     code = _strip_comments(_norm(text))\\n194:     for m in re.finditer(r\\\"(?m)^\\\\s*import\\\\s+(\\\\S+)\\\", code):\\n195:         if m.group(1) not in _ALLOWED_IMPORTS:\\n196:             return False, f\\\"import `{m.group(1)}` is not allowed\\\"\\n197:     m = _FORBIDDEN_CMD.search(code)\\n198:     if m:\\n199:         return False, f\\\"forbidden command `{m.group(1)}`\\\"\\n200:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\\n\",\"truncated_fields\":[\"content\"]}\n31: {\"step_index\":74,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:26Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"300\",\"StartLine\":\"200\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing file\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View antigravity_handoff.py lines 200-300\\\"\"}}]}\n32: {\"step_index\":75,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:29Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:46:29+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:46:29+02:00\\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py`\\nTotal Lines: 348\\nTotal Bytes: 15551\\nShowing lines 200 to 300\\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\\n200:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\\n201:     if m:\\n202:         return False, f\\\"forbidden construct `{m.group(0)}`\\\"\\n203:     return True, \\\"\\\"\\n204: \\n205: \\n206: def solution_file(returned: str, ident: str, direction: str):\\n207:     \\\"\\\"\\\"The returned file prepared to check one direction, or None.\\n208: \\n209:     The other direction's untouched `sorry` block is removed, and a declaration\\n210:     is appended that forces Lean to confirm the target has exactly the type\\n211:     asked for — `_root_`-qualified, so a same-named `Candidate` or target in some\\n212:     other namespace cannot stand in for it.\\n213:     \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n214:     ret = _norm(returned)\\n215:     other = \\\"refute\\\" if direction == \\\"prove\\\" else \\\"prove\\\"\\n216:     header = {\\\"prove\\\": rf\\\"(?m)^theorem\\\\s+prove_{ident}\\\\s*:\\\\s*Candidate\\\\s*:=\\\",\\n217:               \\\"refute\\\": rf\\\"(?m)^theorem\\\\s+refute_{ident}\\\\s*:\\\\s*¬\\\\s*Candidate\\\\s*:=\\\"}\\n218:     pristine = {\\\"prove\\\": f\\\"theorem prove_{ident} : Candidate := by\\\\n  sorry\\\",\\n219:                 \\\"refute\\\": f\\\"theorem refute_{ident} : ¬ Candidate := by\\\\n  sorry\\\"}\\n220:     if not re.search(header[direction], _strip_comments(ret)):\\n221:         return None\\n222:     if pristine[direction] in ret:\\n223:         return None\\n224:     body = ret.replace(pristine[other], \\\"\\\")\\n225:     ty = \\\"_root_.Candidate\\\" if direction == \\\"prove\\\" else \\\"¬ _root_.Candidate\\\"\\n226:     return (body.rstrip() + \\\"\\\\n\\\\n-- verifier: the target must have exactly this type\\\\n\\\"\\n227:             f\\\"theorem agf_verify_{direction} : {ty} := _root_.{direction}_{ident}\\\\n\\\")\\n228: \\n229: \\n230: def do_verify(args) \\n<truncated 1390 bytes>\\n)\\n263:             continue\\n264: \\n265:         verified, timed_out = [], False\\n266:         for direction in (\\\"prove\\\", \\\"refute\\\"):\\n267:             sol = solution_file(returned, ident, direction)\\n268:             if sol is None:\\n269:                 continue\\n270:             good, log = lean._run_lean(sol, audit_axioms=True)\\n271:             if good:\\n272:                 verified.append(direction)\\n273:             else:\\n274:                 timed_out = timed_out or is_timeout(log)\\n275:                 rec[f\\\"{direction}_log_tail\\\"] = log.strip()[-800:]\\n276: \\n277:         if len(verified) == 2:\\n278:             # Both `Candidate` and `¬ Candidate` accepted means the environment\\n279:             # proves False. Never report that as a resolution.\\n280:             rec.update(status=\\\"INCONSISTENT\\\", reason=\\\"both directions kernel-verified\\\")\\n281:             counts[\\\"INCONSISTENT\\\"] += 1\\n282:             print(f\\\"  {ident}  !!! BOTH DIRECTIONS VERIFIED — inconsistency, investigate\\\")\\n283:         elif verified:\\n284:             d = verified[0]\\n285:             expected = {\\\"true\\\": \\\"prove\\\", \\\"false\\\": \\\"refute\\\"}.get(str(rec[\\\"label\\\"]).lower())\\n286:             if args.strict and expected and d != expected:\\n287:                 rec.update(status=\\\"wrong_direction\\\", direction=d)\\n288:                 counts[\\\"wrong_direction\\\"] += 1\\n289:                 print(f\\\"  {ident}  WRONG DIRECTION: {d} but label={rec['label']}\\\")\\n290:             else:\\n291:                 rec.update(status=\\\"resolved\\\", direction=d)\\n292:                 counts[\\\"resolved\\\"] += 1\\n293:                 print(f\\\"  {ident}  RESOLVED by {d}\\\")\\n294:         else:\\n295:             status = \\\"timeout\\\" if timed_out else \\\"unresolved\\\"\\n296:             rec.update(status=status)\\n297:             counts[status] += 1\\n298:             print(f\\\"  {ident}  {status}\\\")\\n299:         results.append(rec)\\n300: \\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\\n\",\"truncated_fields\":[\"content\"]}\n33: {\"step_index\":76,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:29Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Preparing the Result File**\\n\\nThe function prepares a file suitable for checking a specific direction. It removes parts irrelevant to the current direction and adds a declaration to ensure type correctness within the Lean environment. The function's core purpose is to validate the solution in one direction by ensuring the type is enforced using the `_root_` qualification.\\n\\n\\n**Evaluating the Truth of Candidate**\\n\\nThe \\\"refute\\\" case must have a specific format, and the \\\"prove\\\" case requires a different, but also specific, format. The question is whether \\\"Candidate\\\" can be false in the cases being considered, or if we are accidentally proving something false. It is necessary to consider if an edge case for `GraphCalc.radius G` or `GraphCalc.totalDominationNumber G` could make Candidate false.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Graph Radius and Domination**\\n\\nExamining the definitions of `radius` and `totalDominationNumber`, it is crucial to determine if `radius G > totalDominationNumber G` is ever possible. Calculating the radius of a path graph $P_4$ yields 2, after manually checking the ball at each vertex and calculating eccentricity. Now, checking the `totalDominationNumber` reveals its definition requires a set $s$ where every vertex is adjacent to a vertex in $s$.\\nFor $P_4$: $0-1-2-3$, a dominating set can be {1, 2}. Therefore the `totalDominationNumber` for $P_4$ is 2, since every vertex has an edge to at least one element in {1, 2}.\\nSince the radius and total domination number for $P_4$ are equal, it's worth exploring the possibilities.\\n\\n\\n**Investigating Graph Properties and Candidate**\\n\\nConsidering the cycle graph $C_4$, both radius and total domination number are 2. Subsequently, examining $C_5$, the radius is 2, while total domination number is 3. This indicates a potential inequality. It needs to be determined if the candidate could be false for any graph.\\n\\nThe Lean environment's \\\"decide\\\" function can evaluate computable expressions like the comparison between radius and total domination number. This is crucial for determinin\\n<truncated 2354 bytes>\\n Distances Within the Set**\\n\\nThe distance between vertices within the total dominating set needs to be analyzed, and it is observed that it could be bounded. This might be used to derive a bound.\\n\\n\\n**Verifying Distance Bound Hypothesis**\\n\\nExamining the conjecture: for any dominating set *S*, is there a vertex *v* where all vertices in *S* are within distance |*S*|-1 of *v*? The question is now posed whether or not that statement is true. A counter-example is tested with $P_8$, $S = \\\\{2, 3, 6, 7\\\\}$, finding dist(2, 7) = 5 > |S| = 4. The vertices 4 and 5 in the path have distances to all elements of *S* that are less than or equal to |S|-1. It turns out that those vertices are NOT in *S*.\\n\\n\\n**Confirming Inequality Proof Strategy**\\n\\nTesting the candidate statement on $P_{12}$, confirming the eccentricity of a vertex within the total dominating set is less than or equal to the size of the total dominating set. The general form of the theorem `radius G ≤ totalDominationNumber G` must be proved.\\nThe statement is equivalent to showing the existence of a vertex with eccentricity less than or equal to the total domination number.\\n\\n**Analyzing Available Lemmas**\\n\\nCurrently, the Lean environment has limited theorems available related to the `radius` and `totalDominationNumber`. The existing `GraphInvariantsComputable.lean` contains very few applicable theorems. The need for new, custom lemmas is clear.\\n\\n\\n**Re-evaluating Problem's Intent**\\n\\nThe goal is to prove or refute the candidate statement. Refutation involves finding a specific graph where the statement is false using the \\\"decide\\\" function. The formalization might have bugs, so only solutions accepted by the kernel under audit are counted as correct. Exploiting edge cases for a refutation is a valid approach.\\n\\n**Considering Refutation Approach**\\n\\nThe refutation strategy must identify a graph where the radius is strictly greater than the total domination number. Previous analysis focused on proving the inequality but now needs to explore possible refutation graphs.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"prove_\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search for prove_ across project\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n34: {\"step_index\":78,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:47:08Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"theorem prove_\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/benchmark\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep prove_ in benchmark\\\"\"}}]}\n35: {\"step_index\":79,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:47:11Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:47:11+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:47:11+02:00\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/benchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_8ca1b27c.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":15,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"theorem prove_8ca1b27c : Candidate := by\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/benchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_9e476a83.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":15,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"theorem prove_9e476a83 : Candidate := by\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/benchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_1facfd37.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":15,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"theorem prove_1facfd37 : Candidate := by\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/benchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_e20263a9.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":15,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"theorem prove_e20263a9 : Candidate := by\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/be