`G.Colorable 2`, which has no\\n78:     # Decidable instance; validated against graphcalc before being listed.\\n79:     \\\"bipartite\\\":      \\\"GraphCalc.IsBipartiteClass\\\",\\n80: }\\n81: \\n82: # Every invariant is a minimum/maximum over subsets of V, so evaluation is\\n83: # exponential in the vertex count, and past some size the kernel check stops\\n84: # being worth attempting. The refutation itself is still recorded either way.\\n85: #\\n86: # The previous value of 8 turned out to measure the *heartbeat limit* rather\\n87: # than feasibility: order-9 witnesses all failed at ~26 s with `maxHeartbeats`\\n88: # exceeded, but one verified in 387 s once heartbeats were unbounded (see the\\n89: # preamble). 10 is deliberately conservative — beyond it the cost has not been\\n90: # measured, and an unbounded heartbeat count means a bad case hangs until the\\n91: # subprocess timeout rather than failing fast.\\n92: MAX_WITNESS_ORDER = 10\\n93: \\n94: _REL_LEAN = {\\\"≤\\\": \\\"≤\\\", \\\"≥\\\": \\\"≥\\\", \\\"<=\\\": \\\"≤\\\", \\\">=\\\": \\\"≥\\\", \\\"=\\\": \\\"=\\\"}\\n95: \\n96: # `maxRecDepth` raises the *elaborator's* recursion limit only; it does not\\n97: # weaken the kernel check that follows. The whole proof is evaluation, and the\\n98: # class tests enumerate vertex tuples: the cograph test is four nested\\n99: # quantifiers, so an 8-vertex witness needs 8⁴ steps. Measured at n = 8 — the\\n100: # default depth fails on cograph (connected/tree/claw-free still pass), this\\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\\n\",\"truncated_fields\":[\"content\"]}\n10: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
